{"abstract":"This one-day workshop brings together Brazilian anthropologist Priscila Santos da Costa and Italian artist Sergio Racanati, to explore, both critically and creatively, different research practices engaging the forest, and more particularly the Amazon forest, as a multidimensional articulation – imaginary, real, cultural and economic. The work of Priscila Santos da Costa, based at IT University in Copenhagen, analyzes the intricate relationships between technoscientific approaches to the forest and its resources, often branded as sustainable, and capitalist modes of extraction of materials and knowledges. The question of epistemic extractivism – and the shaping of imaginative modes of relationship with the forest and its human and beyond-human inhabitants – is also at stake in Sergio Racanati’s artistic research around the notion of ‘debris’. In 2022, thanks to the support of the Italian Council, the artist spent a period of time working in the Amazon region, close to the city of Santarém, in the Brazilian state of Pará.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Santos da Costa, Priscila"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-764508"],"filters":{"added":"2024-11","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Amazon Forest","Art","Science","Sustainability","Technology"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"imaginários socio tecnológicos e a amazônia brasileira socio technical imaginaries and the brazilian amazon","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-764508","ids":["itu-85af040b-fd43-44e5-970a-7a05fd1bf61d"],"keyword":["amazon forest","art","science","sustainability","technology"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-A193541019425A913D61E620B7074ACD"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Climate IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Priscila","key":"santosdacostapau","last":"Santos da Costa","locid":"9734ef6f-09d1-4ca1-b968-6ad34872d504","name":"Santos da Costa, Priscila","orcid":"0000-0003-2439-9960","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-85af040b-fd43-44e5-970a-7a05fd1bf61d"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Imaginários Socio-tecnológicos e a Amazônia Brasileira : Socio-technical imaginaries and the Brazilian Amazon","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-764508","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"This paper focuses on the initial phase of the Covid-19 pandemic in Papua New Guinea (March to September 2020), and the government’s response to the crisis. In examining on the one hand inadequate infrastructures, an insufficient healt system, law enforcement agencies afflicted by corruption and the us of brutality, and on the other the customs and traditions of Papua New Guineans, the authors conclude that the lockdowns and social distancing measures implemented around the world are not likely to be successful in Papua New Guinea. They point out that the Covid-19 issue in Papua New Guinea reveals an «already there» state of precarity that makes the distinction «now» and «then» of affluent countries meaningless. The normalcy that characterizes liberal democratic capitalism, or life without instability or precarity, is not normalcy in Papua New Guinea, where indeterminacy is less the exception than the prevailing condition of the times. At least Covid-19 has brought these inequalities starkly into view.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Santos da Costa, Priscila","Kenema, Simon"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382040"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of St Andrews"],"keyword":["COVID 19 pandemic","Government response","Infrastructure inadequacy","Papua New Guinea","Precarity"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"precarity and the state the arrival of covid 000000000019 in papua new guinea","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dw","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86796020/14_kenema_dacosta.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382040","ids":["itu-34b58ca8-cb3c-4163-9f1b-7773588236e6"],"keyword":["Covid-19 pandemic","Government response","Infrastructure inadequacy","Papua New Guinea","Precarity"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-88D606741707F131FEDBA5E767682D89"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86796020/14_kenema_dacosta.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of st andrews","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of St Andrews","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Priscila","key":"santosdacostapau","last":"Santos da Costa","locid":"9734ef6f-09d1-4ca1-b968-6ad34872d504","name":"Santos da Costa, Priscila","orcid":"0000-0003-2439-9960","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Simon","key":"kenemapau","last":"Kenema","name":"Kenema, Simon","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"6","status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-34b58ca8-cb3c-4163-9f1b-7773588236e6"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Precarity and the State: : The arrival of covid-19 in Papua New Guinea","type":"Working Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382040","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"In a time of rapid global change, the question of what determines patterns in species abundance distribution remains a priority for understanding the complex dynamics of ecosystems. The constrained maximization of information entropy provides a framework for the understanding of such complex systems dynamics by a quantitative analysis of important constraints via predictions using least biased probability distributions. We apply it to over two thousand hectares of Amazonian tree inventories across seven forest types and thirteen functional traits, representing major global axes of plant strategies. Results show that constraints formed by regional relative abundances of genera explain eight times more of local relative abundances than constraints based on directional selection for specific functional traits, although the latter does show clear signals of environmental dependency. These results provide a quantitative insight by inference from large-scale data using cross-disciplinary methods, furthering our understanding of ecological dynamics.","author_count":216,"contributors":["Pos, Edwin","de Souza Coelho, Luiz","de Andrade Lima Filho, Diogenes","Salomão, Rafael P.","Amaral, Iêda Leão","de Almeida Matos, Francisca Dionízia","Castilho, Carolina V.","Phillips, Oliver L.","Guevara, Juan Ernesto","de Jesus Veiga Carim, Marcelo","López, Dairon Cárdenas","Magnusson, William E.","Wittmann, Florian","Irume, Mariana Victória","Martins, Maria Pires","Sabatier, Daniel","da Silva Guimarães, José Renan","Molino, Jean François","Bánki, Olaf S.","Piedade, Maria Teresa Fernandez","Pitman, Nigel C.A.","Mendoza, Abel Monteagudo","Ramos, José Ferreira","Hawes, Joseph E.","Almeida, Everton José","Barbosa, Luciane Ferreira","Cavalheiro, Larissa","dos Santos, Márcia Cléia Vilela","Luize, Bruno Garcia","de Leão Novo, Evlyn Márcia Moraes","Vargas, Percy Núñez","Silva, Thiago Sanna Freire","Venticinque, Eduardo Martins","Manzatto, Angelo Gilberto","Reis, Neidiane Farias Costa